WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Claimant: Hi, I came home and found that a burst supply pipe had leaked onto the kitchen floor and water had damaged my personal belongings.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.100
Adjuster: How soon after you found the water damage did you first report it to your insurer?

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:18.750
Claimant: I reported it 44 days after I discovered the damage. I was hospitalized for most of that time.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:28.650
Adjuster: I'm sorry you were hospitalized. Were the burst pipe and damaged belongings still available for inspection when you reported the loss?

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.300
Claimant: I don't know if the pipe and belongings were still available for inspection when I reported it.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:01:07.000
Adjuster: Decision: requires further review. The reported burst supply pipe damaging personal belongings fits the accidental plumbing discharge peril in CA-HO4-WATER. Notice was given 44 days after discovery, but whether that delay breached the prompt-notice duty and prejudiced the insurer under CA-HO4-NOTICE remains unresolved. Next action: Clarify the circumstances of delayed reporting and determine whether the delay impaired inspection or otherwise prejudiced the insurer before issuing a final coverage determination.
